# Debugging an assembly issue

Turns out the following 4 instructions don't all generate the same assembly
code when using the `as86` assembler from the dev86 toolchain:

movb	al,*7
movb	al,0x07
movb	al,#0x07
movb	al,#7

The opcodes generated are:

B0 07       movb al,*7
A0 00 07    movb al,0x07 (interpreted as `mov al,[0x0007]`)
B0 07       movb al,#0x07
B0 07       movb al,#7

In particular, note that the `0x07` literal in being interpreted as a memory
address rather than a numeric constant literal. This results in a different
8088 instruction. Both as86 and MINIX's asld behave this way.
